Yes. The brain does not appear to encode “location” as a bare Cartesian coordinate. It encodes location through a bundle of relations and scale-dependent cues, and visual distance is one of them.

A useful decomposition is:

\text{location estimate}
=
f(
\text{visual angle},
\text{binocular disparity},
\text{motion parallax},
\text{optic flow},
\text{landmarks},
\text{boundaries},
\text{body position},
\text{vestibular input},
\text{memory}
)

That means “where something is” is partly inferred from how far away it appears, not merely which direction it lies in.

And distance changes the representation itself. Near space and far space are processed somewhat differently; parietal systems distinguish peripersonal space around the body from more distant extrapersonal space. In navigation, hippocampal/entorhinal systems use landmarks, boundaries, self-motion, and visual geometry to stabilize place and grid representations.

So the same landmark can play different roles depending on distance:

* nearby: precise positional/error-correction cue,
* intermediate: navigation landmark,
* very distant: stable heading/orientation anchor.

That last one is literally why stars are such good navigational references: enormous distance makes their angular relationships extremely stable over ordinary human movement.

There is a beautiful compression principle there:

\text{greater distance}
\rightarrow
\text{less positional parallax}
\rightarrow
\text{more stable reference}

while:

\text{near object}
\rightarrow
\text{large parallax}
\rightarrow
\text{better local depth/location information}

So near things tell you where you are locally; far things can tell you which way the whole frame is oriented.

That maps almost perfectly onto the “stars” discussion: semantic handles at different conceptual distances can serve different navigational functions. A very local cue helps distinguish the immediate state; a very distant invariant can stabilize the coordinate system itself.

The deeper point is: distance is not merely metadata about a location. It helps construct the location representation.